From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] cellKey2char: the SDK's API; sys_net offline: bind, getsockname, inet_pton

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic, no title names)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0025)

What:
- libs/misc/cellKey2char.c declared invented signatures: Open(arrangement,
  handle*), with a u32 index as the handle, and KeyCodeToChar/GetMode, which
  no title imports. The SDK passes the address of the title's own 128-byte
  handle buffer to Open, Close, GetChar, SetMode and SetArrangement. A title's
  Open(handle) was therefore read with its second register as the
  out-pointer, and the index was written through whatever that register held
  (guest memory corruption); SetArrangement (0x0DFBADFA) and GetChar were not
  registered at all. The module now has the SDK's five calls with RPCS3's
  answers (behaviour only; no RPCS3 code) and RPCS3's error values
  (0x80121301-06; the header had 0x801216xx): NULL handle INVALID_HANDLE, byte
  8 of the buffer the open flag (ALREADY_INITIALIZED / UNINITIALIZED), byte 0
  == 255 OTHER, mode at most NATIVE2 and arrangement 0..24 (INVALID_PARAMETER).
  Where RPCS3 has a TODO: Open sets the flag and Close clears it,
  SetArrangement keeps the arrangement in byte 1, and GetChar converts
  nothing (*charNum 0, *processed false), since no keyboard is attached.
- runtime/ppu/ppu_sysprx.cpp's sys_net offline model (the default without
  PS3_NET_ONLINE) had no getsockname (0x13EFE7F5) or inet_pton (0x8AF3825E):
  both took the unresolved path, CELL_OK with nothing written. bind answered
  0 and kept nothing. New runtime/ppu/ppu_net_offline.h: bind records the
  address per socket (an ephemeral port from 49152 for port 0, EINVAL for a
  second bind), getsockname reports it (0.0.0.0:0 when unbound) and sets the
  length to 16, writing at most *addrlen bytes; inet_pton parses AF_INET
  dotted quads (1 and the address, 0 and dst untouched otherwise, -1 with
  EAFNOSUPPORT for another family). Errors are -1 with errno (EBADF, EFAULT).
  _sys_net_errno_loc no longer overwrites errno with EWOULDBLOCK on every
  call; it sets that once, when it allocates the cell.

Why: a title that opens a key-to-character handle at boot had guest memory
overwritten, and one that reads back its bound socket's address or parses an
address literal got garbage or "invalid".

Test: libs/misc/tests/test_key2char.c (tools/test_key2char.py) checks the
NIDs, that Open writes only inside the handle buffer, and every answer above.
runtime/ppu/tests/test_net_offline.cpp (tools/test_net_offline.py) checks
bind and getsockname (bound, unbound, port 0, short length, EBADF, EFAULT) and
inet_pton on valid and invalid literals.

diff --git a/libs/misc/cellKey2char.c b/libs/misc/cellKey2char.c
--- a/libs/misc/cellKey2char.c
+++ b/libs/misc/cellKey2char.c
@@ -1,206 +1,83 @@
 /*
  * ps3recomp - cellKey2char HLE implementation
  *
- * Maps HID keyboard scancodes to ASCII/Unicode characters.
- * Implements US-101 layout; other layouts return the same mapping
- * (sufficient for most recompiled games).
+ * The SDK's API: cellKey2CharOpen(handle), Close(handle), GetChar(handle,
+ * kdata, charCode, charNum, processed), SetMode(handle, mode) and
+ * SetArrangement(handle, arrange), where handle is the guest address of the
+ * title's 128-byte handle buffer. The module used to declare invented
+ * signatures (Open(arrangement, handle*), a u32 index as the handle), so a
+ * title's Open(handle) was read with its second register as the out-pointer
+ * and a small index was written through whatever that register held.
+ *
+ * Behaviour follows RPCS3's cellKey2char (behaviour only; no RPCS3 code):
+ * NULL handle INVALID_HANDLE; byte 8 of the buffer is the open flag
+ * (ALREADY_INITIALIZED, UNINITIALIZED); byte 0 == 255 is OTHER; the mode must
+ * be at most NATIVE2 and the arrangement 0..24 (INVALID_PARAMETER). Where
+ * RPCS3 leaves a TODO this module does the plain thing: Open sets the flag
+ * (RPCS3 sets nothing, so every later call there answers UNINITIALIZED), Close
+ * clears it, SetArrangement keeps the arrangement in byte 1, and GetChar
+ * converts nothing (*charNum 0, *processed false), because no keyboard is
+ * attached (cellKb reports none).
  */
